  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nA weak shortwave trough continues to pivot across northeast IL \nand northwest IN this afternoon with a stalled frontal boundary \nhanging around the I-80 corridor. Despite these features, most \nareas have remained dry today aside from a cluster of showers  \ntraversing across IN. These showers will continue to move away \nfrom our area this afternoon. With the front expected to slowly \nmeander south across the area this evening, additional isolated \nto widely scattered showers will likely continue to fester near \nand south of the front through this evening and possibly \novernight as well. While thunder coverage should be low given \nthe modest instability, occasional lightning strikes will be \npossible with any showers especially prior to sunset with about \na 10-15% thunderstorm chance thereafter mainly for areas south \nof the Kankakee River Valley overnight. \n\nOutside of the isolated showers, partly cloudy skies will \nlinger through the night with temperatures cooling slightly into\nthe mid to upper 60s. With more cloud cover forecast to hang \naround tonight, the threat for patchy fog is fairly low. \nHowever, with lingering low-level moisture and the light winds \nsome patchy ground fog cannot be ruled out especially near the \nfront and outside of the urban core of Chicago. Despite the \nlower confidence have opted to introduce some patchy (20% \ncoverage) fog to the forecast for tonight. \n\nDrier conditions are expected for Friday and Saturday as drier \nmid-level air advects in and puts a cap on the atmosphere. \nSkies should also scatter out through the day on Friday with mostly\nsunny skies for Friday afternoon and again on Saturday. \nTemperatures will be otherwise seasonable with highs in the \nmid-80s each day. However, there will be a low chance for a \ncouple of lingering showers Friday morning in northwest IN \nfollowed by another low chance for scattered showers and storms \nFriday night as a front moves through. Given that the atmosphere\nshould be capped the expectation is for any showers/storms to \ndissipate prior to reaching northern IL, but have maintained \nsome 20-30% POPs just in case for now. \n  \nThe forecast for Saturday night onward generally remains \nunchanged as a strengthening upper ridge in the southwest CONUS \nwill amplify the northwest flow over the Great Lakes. As a \nresult, periods of thunderstorms are expected to traverse across\nthe Great Lakes as several shortwaves dive through the region. \nThe first period of interest will be late Saturday night into \nSunday where guidance shows the first wave diving through. While\nguidance does show a complex of thunderstorms developing in the\nUpper Midwest and diving towards northern IL, there is a lot of\nvariability as to whether or not the complex will track over us\nor miss us just to the west. Depending on where this complex \ngoes will dictate how Sunday afternoon plays out locally which \nmeans we could get more scattered thunderstorms Sunday afternoon\nor stay dry until the next shortwave arrives on Monday. That \nsaid, the environment overhead Sunday afternoon will be one \nsupportive of severe weather with strong instability and decent \nwind shear forecast to be present. Similarly, Monday afternoon \nalso looks to be another period supportive of severe weather. \nThough confidence on storm timing and coverage Monday afternoon \nremains uncertain and will likely be dictated on how things \nevolve on Sunday. Bottom line, the end of the weekend and \nbeginning of next week looks to start off active so make sure \nyou keep checking back for forecast updates. \n\nAdditional periods of showers and thunderstorms will be \npossible through at least the middle of next week as the \nnorthwest flow remains in place. While there will be some dry \nhours mixed in, confidence on the specifics at this time range \nare low. Regardless, seasonable temperatures in the 80s (with \novernight lows in the 60s) are forecast to prevail through the \nearly part of next week.\n\nYack",
